{"id":"overtime-sheriff-against-peers","tier":"REDFLAG","title":"Sheriff and jails overtime: San Francisco against other large California employers","question":"Is overtime in San Francisco’s sheriff and jails high against the same kind of department elsewhere in California?","finding":"In 2025, San Francisco’s sheriff and jails overtime was 47.6% of regular pay (55,706 dollars per paid position), ranking 1 of 13; the middle of the others was 24.9% (24,258 dollars per position). Highest: San Francisco, Alameda (county), San Mateo (county). In 2019 San Francisco’s share was 30.3%, rank 2 of 13 (middle of the others 18.3%). 106 of its positions were paid more in overtime than in regular pay (111.46 per 1,000 salaried positions).","how":"State Controller raw export. Departments are picked by the name each employer reports (a name containing sheriff, jail, detention, corrections or custody, leaving out health, probation and oversight bodies). Only employer-years with 50 or more paid positions in such departments are shown. Overtime share is overtime divided by regular pay. The export does not say whether a year is a calendar or a fiscal year. San Francisco’s overtime here is $480.3 million for 2024; the City’s own payroll file has $468.9 million for fiscal 2024 and $471.0 million for fiscal 2025, so the two sources are close but not the same and are not mixed in any one figure.","limits":"Department names differ between employers, so a department can be missed or civilian staff counted in. Overtime depends on vacancies, minimum staffing rules set in labor agreements, and paid work for outside events billed to others; a high share is a question about staffing, not proof of abuse. A position is a row, not a person.","columns":[{"k":"employer","t":"Employer","type":"org"},{"k":"year","t":"Year","type":"year"},{"k":"positions","t":"Paid positions","type":"int"},{"k":"otShare","t":"Overtime as share of regular pay","type":"pct"},{"k":"otPer","t":"Overtime per paid position",